It is generally not legal or ethical to access someone else's emails without their permission. Most email services have privacy settings in place which can limit who can see an individual's emails. If this isn't the case, other measures may include:
1. Compromising the security of the email account by using malware or guesswork to gain the login credentials;
2. Email harvesting - collecting valid usernames and passwords from file sharing networks;
3. Social engineering - posing as someone trustworthy and convincing a targeted individual to hand over access;
4. Utilising bugs or backdoors - injected by hackers into websites, software applications, or computers so that stored data can be copied without alerting the user; 5. Litigative force - compelling an individual to provide credentials through legal action such as a subpoena, but only when relevant to a specific court case .

